  "llm_analysis": {
    "hotspots": "Large-scale foundation model training and distributed systems optimization; Multi-modal learning (language, audio, reasoning) with adaptive architectures; Privacy-preserving and causal machine learning; Game-theoretic and economic approaches to multi-agent decision making; Rigorous evaluation benchmarks and discovery-driven methodologies.",
    "trends": "Convergence of scaling laws with system-level efficiency (cs.DC, cs.LG); Integration of reasoning, planning, and causal inference into language models (cs.AI, cs.CL, stat.ML); Rise of adaptive, domain-generalizable models with prior-aware learning (stat.ME, econ.EM); Increased focus on audio-language tasks and multi-task unification (cs.CL, cs.LG); Formal verification and programming language techniques for ML reliability (cs.PL, cs.MS).",
    "future_directions": "Co-design of models and distributed infrastructure for sustainable scaling; Causal and counterfactual reasoning for robust out-of-distribution generalization; Privacy-aware federated and multi-party learning with game-theoretic incentives; Automated scientific discovery loops combining evidence, evaluation, and adaptive experimentation; Unified benchmarks for reasoning, planning, and multi-modal understanding across domains.",
    "analysis_summary": "The current landscape centers on scaling intelligence through system-model co-design, multi-modal reasoning, and trustworthy learning. Keywords reveal a shift from pure performance chasing to adaptive, causal, and privacy-conscious frameworks, with strong cross-pollination between distributed systems, game theory, econometrics, and language technologies. Future progress hinges on unifying these threads into verifiable, generalizable, and socially aligned AI systems."
